(project tuna-call-sites
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: call-sites — path-tagged boundary calls. Every prim call in a compiled program carries its canonical path in the program tree; diagnostics address trees, source spans are a courtesy on top.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section call-sites
  (doc "The seam between compiled trees and human comprehension: canonical program paths as the primary coordinate system, with IR provenance retained so diagnostics can talk about source.")

  (subsection tagging
   (doc "Callsite paths assigned at compile time")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         During compilation (surface IR -> tree via bracket abstraction,
         SPEC.md §5), every prim-call node is annotated with its
         canonical path in the compiled program tree: the sequence of
         constructor-descent choices from the root. Because programs are
         content-hash-addressed and immutable, the pair
         (program_hash, path) globally identifies a callsite. Journal
         rows reference callsites by exactly this pair
         (journal.row-schema).

         Trees are illegible to humans and the spec does not pretend
         otherwise (SPEC.md §5 exists precisely to compensate); the path
         IS the coordinate system every downstream surface
         (replay.divergence-surface, grants.audit, the REPL's
         structural debugger) agrees on. Tooling argument: paths are
         grep-able, indexable, diff-able — addressing beats reading.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection stability
   (doc "Path stability across patching and recompilation")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Accepted truth: patches invalidate paths below the edit point —
         a structural patch renames everything beneath it, and queued
         patches referencing old paths go stale (SPEC.md §4.5 CAS makes
         the staleness explicit rather than silent). v1 rules:

           - A callsite path is ONLY meaningful paired with the program
             hash it was compiled under. Surfaces always print the pair.
           - Recompilation of unchanged source MUST produce the same
             program hash AND the same callsite paths for unchanged
             subtrees (compiler determinism is part of SPEC.md §2's
             tested determinism).
           - Path-forwarding ("old path -> new path after patch") is NOT
             attempted in v1. The first-diff between program versions
             (SPEC.md §4.5) plus provenance maps give enough to relocate
             by hand; a relocation tool is a v2 candidate, not assumed.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection provenance
   (doc "Mapping tree paths back to source spans: the UX-bearing invariant")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The compiler retains, per compiled artifact, a provenance map:
         callsite tree path -> IR path -> source span (SPEC.md §5:
         "diagnostics address programs by tree path; source spans are a
         human courtesy only"). Bracket abstraction decorrelates tree
         shape from source shape — this decorrelation is exactly where
         debugging UX lives or dies.

         Rules: every diagnostic (denial, divergence, fuel exhaustion)
         carries the callsite pair AND, when the provenance map is
         available, the source span; a diagnostic that cannot say where
         it came from is a bug report against the compiler, not against
         the user. Optimizations that fold/duplicate callsites must
         maintain the map through the transform or be gated off by
         default — same engine-split discipline as replay.faithful's
         step-count ruling. This subsection is the spec location of the
         "debugging the compiled tree by hand = v1 failed the UX bar"
         failure definition (acceptance.failure-definitions).
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
